PATCH SKETCH (not applied; Proximum fork seantempesta/proximum @ 9846d3e).
Owner: Proximum fork. Lands only in a root restart batch (fork bump).

Goal: sync! cost proportional to what the commit changed (dirty vector chunk +
dirty edge chunks + PSS nodes), never to the mmap file. Konserve chunks are the
source of truth (upstream design, proximum.vectors ns docstring); the mmap is a
per-owner cache. 9846d3e made every sync! copy the whole capacity-sized mmap
(`proximum.vectors/publish-mmap-generation!`) and SHA-256 it three times
(`file-evidence` on source, temporary and destination). That is the measured
O(capacity * dim) per-commit cost. Replace "an immutable copy of the bytes" with
"an immutable name for the bytes": the vectors address map already names every
chunk by an immutable konserve key, so a working mmap is valid for a commit iff
its loaded prefix's chunk addresses equal the commit's.

--- a/src/proximum/hnsw.clj   (p/sync! for HnswIndex)
@@
-                                       mmap-generation
-                                       (when-let [mmap-dir (:mmap-dir state)]
-                                         (vectors/publish-mmap-generation!
-                                          (:mmap-path vs) mmap-dir commit-id
-                                          (:reflink-supported? state)))
-                                       snapshot (cond-> snapshot
-                                                  mmap-generation
-                                                  (assoc :mmap-generation commit-id ...))]
+                                       ;; The commit names its vectors by the address PSS root it
+                                       ;; already stores; the working mmap records which chunk
+                                       ;; addresses it holds (header bytes 32-63 + a sidecar of the
+                                       ;; chunk address vector), O(dirty chunks) to update.
+                                       _ (vectors/record-loaded-addresses! vs @(:chunk-address-map vs))]

--- a/src/proximum/vectors.clj
+(defn record-loaded-addresses!
+  "Write the chunk addresses this mmap holds beside it (vectors-<owner>.addrs),
+   replacing only entries for chunks flushed since the last sync."
+  [vs address-map] ...)
+
+(defn reusable-prefix
+  "Number of leading chunks of an existing mmap whose recorded addresses equal
+   `address-map`'s; those chunks need no load."
+  [mmap-path address-map] ...)
 (defn open-store* ...
-        start-chunk (if mmap-compatible? (chunk-id mmap-header-count chunk-size) 0)
+        start-chunk (if mmap-compatible? (reusable-prefix actual-mmap-path address-map) 0)

Restore of any commit (historical, other branch, guarded force) then loads only
the chunks whose addresses differ: O(changed chunks) when the owner's mmap is
the same lineage, O(N) only for a cold or diverged owner, and never at commit.
`materialize-working-mmap!`, `publish-mmap-generation!`, `file-sha256`,
`verify-generation!` and `sweep-mmap-generations!` are deleted with their
callers (force-branch! materializes through open-store* like load-commit).
Snapshots written by 9846d3e keep loading: an old :mmap-generation key is ignored
and its chunks are loaded from konserve.

Proof: proximum.branching-test (guarded force, historical restore) green; a
sync! timing assertion at N=10k and N=100k, dim 768, shows 1-vector commit cost
flat in N and capacity (measured parent: 270 ms at N=1k cap 20k, 700 ms at
N=10k cap 110k; see report.md).

Second, smaller sizing change (same owner): PersistentEdgeIndex CHUNK_SIZE 1024
nodes => one layer-0 chunk is ~135 KB at M0=32, and one inserted vector dirties
up to ~M0 neighbour chunks, so a 1-vector commit rewrites ~1.3 MB at N=10k
(measured) and up to ~4 MB at N>=100k. A 64-node chunk bounds that to
~M0 * 8.4 KB = ~270 KB. Measure before choosing; it is a constant, not a design
change.

Reconciled with lane embed-model (embedding/report.md, ce071b61e): its suggested
fix keeps the generation file but takes the evidence from sync!'s chunk hash
chain, with full verification only on restore/audit. That removes the hashing,
but not the copy: on a filesystem without reflink (and through stock `cp
--reflink` on macOS), every commit still writes a capacity-sized file. This
sketch goes one step further. The evidence is the chunk address map (immutable
konserve keys, hashes under :crypto-hash?), and no generation file is written,
so commit cost does not depend on the filesystem.
